# Everything outside the course tree that changes on a WSL-looking machine whose only
# missing piece is systemd in wsl.conf.
#
#   /etc/wsl.conf   the change -- but see below, `sandbox arrange` sets the starting state at
#                   boot, so the path appears whichever way the installer went and it is the
#                   CONTENT report that distinguishes the four states
#   /work           the read-only mount this harness adds, not the installer
#   the bare dirs   mtimes
#
# Notably ABSENT and worth noticing: no /etc/passwd, no /etc/shadow, no /etc/subuid, and no
# podman store -- setup_wslconf is two file writes and touches nothing else, and this case asks
# for --fake-podman so nothing real runs. That is the claim.
#
# ALSO ABSENT, and it is a fixture property rather than luck: the fake podman is bind-mounted
# over /usr/bin/podman, which already exists in the image, so it adds no path here. Mounting it
# at /usr/local/bin/podman instead put it in this list as a change the installer had made.
#
# AND /tmp IS LISTED, WHICH IS NOT ABOUT PODMAN AT ALL. This line was briefly removed on the
# macOS branch with the reasoning that a REAL podman writes /tmp/containers-user-$UID and
# /tmp/podman-run-$UID on first run -- true, and it is why expected-system-paths.podman-old
# claims it -- so a --fake-podman case could not reach /tmp. That reasoning was sound and the
# measurement behind it was not: this case runs the installer to completion, which calls the
# LAUNCHER three times (installer:1044 --rebuild, :1101 --dev-print-command, :1120 doctor),
# and --rebuild writes METER_STATE, BUILD_LOG and run_timeout's scratch into ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}
# whichever podman is on PATH. TMPDIR is not forwarded into these containers, so it is unset
# in here -- and at the time it was measured, an unguarded `${TMPDIR%/}` under `set -u` killed
# the launcher on its 152nd line before it could write any of them. /tmp stayed clean because
# the launcher was dead, so removing the line recorded the bug as the expectation.
#
# The lesson is the one the fixture is for: a path DISAPPEARING from a diff is evidence about
# the code, and "we no longer touch it" and "we no longer get that far" look identical here.
A /etc/wsl.conf
A /work
C /etc
C /home
C /home/student
C /tmp
